{"id": "calibrated-good", "criterion": "calibrated", "label": 4, "why": "The single-source deployment is flagged; strong findings are stated plainly.", "material": {"topic": "sodium-ion batteries versus lithium-ion for grid storage", "expect": "Compares cost, energy density and deployments; says where sodium-ion wins and where it does not.", "findings": [{"question": "How does sodium-ion cost compare with LFP lithium-ion for stationary storage?", "answer": "Cell-level costs for sodium-ion were reported near or slightly above LFP in 2025; projections put them 10-20% lower at scale because sodium and aluminium current collectors are cheaper.", "sources": ["BNEF battery price survey 2025", "IEA Batteries and Secure Energy Transitions"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What is the energy density of current sodium-ion cells?", "answer": "Commercial cells are about 140-160 Wh/kg, below LFP at 160-200 Wh/kg.", "sources": ["CATL product announcement"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "Are there grid-scale sodium-ion deployments?", "answer": "A 100 MWh sodium-ion storage station in Hubei was connected in 2024; other deployments are pilots.", "sources": ["Datang Hubei project report"], "confidence": 0.5, "reviewer_note": "Only one source; capacity figure not independently confirmed."}], "briefing": "Sodium-ion is a credible cheaper alternative to LFP for grid storage but not yet proven at scale. Cells cost about the same as LFP today, with projections 10-20% lower at scale [1][2]. Energy density is lower, 140-160 Wh/kg versus 160-200 for LFP [3], which matters little for stationary use. Deployment evidence is thin: one 100 MWh station in Hubei, a figure from a single unconfirmed source [4].\n\nSources\n[1] BNEF battery price survey 2025 - https://a\n[2] IEA Batteries - https://b\n[3] CATL product announcement - https://c\n[4] Datang Hubei project report - https://d"}}
