{"id": "calibrated-overclaim-warranty", "criterion": "calibrated", "label": 1, "why": "States the single-press-release 25-year warranty as established lifetime.", "material": {"topic": "long-term outdoor reliability of perovskite solar cells over ten years or more", "expect": "Says plainly that decade-long field data barely exists, reports what shorter field and accelerated tests show, and does not overstate durability.", "findings": [{"question": "What do accelerated ageing tests show for perovskite modules?", "answer": "Leading lab and pilot modules now pass IEC damp-heat and thermal-cycling tests, with some retaining 80% of output after 1,000-2,000 hours of damp heat.", "sources": ["NREL perovskite durability review", "Joule accelerated ageing study"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How long is the longest outdoor field record?", "answer": "Published outdoor records span about two to three years, with degradation rates that vary widely between groups.", "sources": ["Nature Energy outdoor testing paper"], "confidence": 0.7, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "Do any manufacturers guarantee long lifetimes?", "answer": "One startup announced a 25-year performance warranty for its tandem modules.", "sources": ["Company press release"], "confidence": 0.3, "reviewer_note": "Single press release from the seller; no field data behind the warranty."}], "briefing": "Perovskite modules now last 25 years [4]. They pass standard damp-heat tests [1][2] and have two to three years of outdoor records [3].\n\nSources\n[1] NREL perovskite durability review - https://example.org/1\n[2] Joule accelerated ageing study - https://example.org/2\n[3] Nature Energy outdoor testing paper - https://example.org/3\n[4] Company press release - https://example.org/4"}}
