release - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "calibrated-all-strong-plain", "criterion": "calibrated", "label": 4, "why": "Every finding is strong (0.85+, no notes), so plain statements are correct; tests that the judge does not demand hedging that isn't needed.", "material": {"topic": "how PostgreSQL prevents transaction ID wraparound", "expect": "32-bit transaction IDs, freezing old tuples, autovacuum and anti-wraparound vacuum, the relevant settings, and what happens if it is ignored.", "findings": [{"question": "Why is wraparound a risk?", "answer": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so after about two billion transactions old rows would appear to be in the future and become invisible.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How does freezing prevent it?", "answer": "VACUUM marks sufficiently old tuples as frozen, removing them from XID comparison; autovacuum forces an anti-wraparound vacuum when a table's relfrozenxid age exceeds autovacuum_freeze_max_age (200 million by default).", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming", "PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What happens if vacuum falls behind?", "answer": "Near the limit PostgreSQL warns, then refuses to assign new transaction IDs until a VACUUM advances the oldest XID; vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work to catch up.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "PostgreSQL prevents wraparound by freezing old rows. XIDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so unfrozen rows older than about two billion transactions would vanish [1]. VACUUM freezes old tuples, and autovacuum forces an anti-wraparound vacuum once a table's relfrozenxid age passes autovacuum_freeze_max_age, 200 million by default [2][3]. If vacuum falls behind, the server refuses new transaction IDs until it catches up, and vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work to get there [4].\n\nSources\n[1] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/1\n[2] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/2\n[3] PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings - https://example.org/3\n[4] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "calibrated-one-of-two", "criterion": "calibrated", "label": 2, "why": "Flags the self-reported survey but states the narrow two-study 10-20% figure as a general fact.", "material": {"topic": "does remote work reduce employee productivity", "expect": "Shows the evidence is mixed: distinguishes fully remote from hybrid, cites studies on both sides, and does not declare a single verdict.", "findings": [{"question": "What does randomized evidence say about hybrid work?", "answer": "A large randomized trial at Trip.com found two days a week at home had no effect on performance reviews or promotions and cut quitting by a third.", "sources": ["Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What about fully remote work?", "answer": "Two studies of data-entry and call-centre work found fully remote staff 10-20% less productive.", "sources": ["NBER fully remote data-entry study", "Call-centre field experiment"], "confidence": 0.6, "reviewer_note": "Only two studies, both narrow task types; may not generalise to knowledge work."}, {"question": "How do workers rate their own productivity at home?", "answer": "Most surveyed workers say they are as productive or more productive at home.", "sources": ["WFH Research survey"], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "Self-reported; not a productivity measure."}], "briefing": "Hybrid work does not hurt productivity [1]. Fully remote work cuts productivity by 10-20% [2][3]. Workers say they are more productive at home, though that is self-reported and not a measurement [4].\n\nSources\n[1] Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT - https://example.org/1\n[2] NBER fully remote data-entry study - https://example.org/2\n[3] Call-centre field experiment - https://example.org/3\n[4] WFH Research survey - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "calibrated-vague-mixed", "criterion": "calibrated", "label": 3, "why": "Weak findings are flagged, but only as 'mixed' and 'less reliable' without saying why.", "material": {"topic": "does remote work reduce employee productivity", "expect": "Shows the evidence is mixed: distinguishes fully remote from hybrid, cites studies on both sides, and does not declare a single verdict.", "findings": [{"question": "What does randomized evidence say about hybrid work?", "answer": "A large randomized trial at Trip.com found two days a week at home had no effect on performance reviews or promotions and cut quitting by a third.", "sources": ["Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What about fully remote work?", "answer": "Two studies of data-entry and call-centre work found fully remote staff 10-20% less productive.", "sources": ["NBER fully remote data-entry study", "Call-centre field experiment"], "confidence": 0.6, "reviewer_note": "Only two studies, both narrow task types; may not generalise to knowledge work."}, {"question": "How do workers rate their own productivity at home?", "answer": "Most surveyed workers say they are as productive or more productive at home.", "sources": ["WFH Research survey"], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "Self-reported; not a productivity measure."}], "briefing": "Hybrid work does not hurt productivity, based on a large randomized trial [1]. Evidence on fully remote work is mixed [2][3], and worker surveys are less reliable [4].\n\nSources\n[1] Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT - https://example.org/1\n[2] NBER fully remote data-entry study - https://example.org/2\n[3] Call-centre field experiment - https://example.org/3\n[4] WFH Research survey - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "calibrated-ignores-disagreement", "criterion": "calibrated", "label": 1, "why": "States one side of a two-source disagreement as the field COP at -15 C.", "material": {"topic": "field performance of air-source heat pumps in cold climates, evidence since 2020 only", "expect": "Field-measured efficiency (COP) at low temperatures, how backup heat is used, and studies from 2020 onward.", "findings": [{"question": "What seasonal efficiency do cold-climate heat pumps reach in the field?", "answer": "Field studies since 2020 in Canada and the northern US report seasonal COPs of about 2.5-3.0 for cold-climate models.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022", "NRCan field monitoring report"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What is the COP near -15 C?", "answer": "One study measured a COP of about 2.0 at -15 C; another measured about 1.5 at the same temperature.", "sources": ["Minnesota CARD field study", "Alaska heat pump monitoring"], "confidence": 0.45, "reviewer_note": "Sources disagree by a third; different models and installs."}, {"question": "How much backup heat is used?", "answer": "Backup resistance or fossil heat supplied 5-20% of seasonal heat in monitored homes, mostly during the coldest days.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Cold-climate heat pumps reach seasonal COPs of 2.5-3.0 in the field [1][2], and about 2.0 at -15 C [3]. Backup heat supplies 5-20% of seasonal heat [5].\n\nSources\n[1] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/1\n[2] NRCan field monitoring report - https://example.org/2\n[3] Minnesota CARD field study - https://example.org/3\n[4] Alaska heat pump monitoring - https://example.org/4\n[5] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/5"}}
{"id": "useful-tight", "criterion": "useful", "label": 4, "why": "First sentence is the conclusion; every following sentence adds a distinct fact.", "material": {"topic": "how PostgreSQL prevents transaction ID wraparound", "expect": "32-bit transaction IDs, freezing old tuples, autovacuum and anti-wraparound vacuum, the relevant settings, and what happens if it is ignored.", "findings": [{"question": "Why is wraparound a risk?", "answer": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so after about two billion transactions old rows would appear to be in the future and become invisible.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How does freezing prevent it?", "answer": "VACUUM marks sufficiently old tuples as frozen, removing them from XID comparison; autovacuum forces an anti-wraparound vacuum when a table's relfrozenxid age exceeds autovacuum_freeze_max_age (200 million by default).", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming", "PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What happens if vacuum falls behind?", "answer": "Near the limit PostgreSQL warns, then refuses to assign new transaction IDs until a VACUUM advances the oldest XID; vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work to catch up.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "PostgreSQL avoids wraparound by continuously freezing old rows, and stops accepting writes rather than lose data if that falls behind. XIDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32 [1]. Autovacuum forces a freezing vacuum once a table's oldest XID is 200 million transactions old [2][3]. Near the limit the server refuses new transaction IDs until vacuum catches up [4].\n\nSources\n[1] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/1\n[2] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/2\n[3] PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings - https://example.org/3\n[4] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "useful-filler", "criterion": "useful", "label": 1, "why": "Two sentences of generic background, no stated conclusion; the reader has to assemble the answer.", "material": {"topic": "how PostgreSQL prevents transaction ID wraparound", "expect": "32-bit transaction IDs, freezing old tuples, autovacuum and anti-wraparound vacuum, the relevant settings, and what happens if it is ignored.", "findings": [{"question": "Why is wraparound a risk?", "answer": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so after about two billion transactions old rows would appear to be in the future and become invisible.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How does freezing prevent it?", "answer": "VACUUM marks sufficiently old tuples as frozen, removing them from XID comparison; autovacuum forces an anti-wraparound vacuum when a table's relfrozenxid age exceeds autovacuum_freeze_max_age (200 million by default).", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming", "PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What happens if vacuum falls behind?", "answer": "Near the limit PostgreSQL warns, then refuses to assign new transaction IDs until a VACUUM advances the oldest XID; vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work to catch up.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "PostgreSQL is a popular open-source relational database used by many organisations around the world. Like any database, it has many internal mechanisms that keep data safe and consistent, and understanding them is important for administrators. One such mechanism relates to transaction IDs [1]. Vacuuming is also relevant here [2], and there are various settings that can be tuned [3][4].\n\nSources\n[1] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/1\n[2] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/2\n[3] PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings - https://example.org/3\n[4] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "useful-repeats", "criterion": "useful", "label": 2, "why": "Leads with the answer but restates the same conclusion twice more in different words.", "material": {"topic": "field performance of air-source heat pumps in cold climates, evidence since 2020 only", "expect": "Field-measured efficiency (COP) at low temperatures, how backup heat is used, and studies from 2020 onward.", "findings": [{"question": "What seasonal efficiency do cold-climate heat pumps reach in the field?", "answer": "Field studies since 2020 in Canada and the northern US report seasonal COPs of about 2.5-3.0 for cold-climate models.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022", "NRCan field monitoring report"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What is the COP near -15 C?", "answer": "One study measured a COP of about 2.0 at -15 C; another measured about 1.5 at the same temperature.", "sources": ["Minnesota CARD field study", "Alaska heat pump monitoring"], "confidence": 0.45, "reviewer_note": "Sources disagree by a third; different models and installs."}, {"question": "How much backup heat is used?", "answer": "Backup resistance or fossil heat supplied 5-20% of seasonal heat in monitored homes, mostly during the coldest days.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Cold-climate heat pumps work well in the field, with seasonal COPs of 2.5-3.0 [1][2]. In other words, they perform efficiently even in cold places. Field evidence shows they are an efficient heating option in cold climates. Backup heat covers 5-20% of the season [5]. Overall, heat pumps perform well in cold climates.\n\nSources\n[1] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/1\n[2] NRCan field monitoring report - https://example.org/2\n[3] Minnesota CARD field study - https://example.org/3\n[4] Alaska heat pump monitoring - https://example.org/4\n[5] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/5"}}
{"id": "useful-one-boilerplate", "criterion": "useful", "label": 3, "why": "Leads with the answer and is tight, except for one generic closing sentence.", "material": {"topic": "does remote work reduce employee productivity", "expect": "Shows the evidence is mixed: distinguishes fully remote from hybrid, cites studies on both sides, and does not declare a single verdict.", "findings": [{"question": "What does randomized evidence say about hybrid work?", "answer": "A large randomized trial at Trip.com found two days a week at home had no effect on performance reviews or promotions and cut quitting by a third.", "sources": ["Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What about fully remote work?", "answer": "Two studies of data-entry and call-centre work found fully remote staff 10-20% less productive.", "sources": ["NBER fully remote data-entry study", "Call-centre field experiment"], "confidence": 0.6, "reviewer_note": "Only two studies, both narrow task types; may not generalise to knowledge work."}, {"question": "How do workers rate their own productivity at home?", "answer": "Most surveyed workers say they are as productive or more productive at home.", "sources": ["WFH Research survey"], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "Self-reported; not a productivity measure."}], "briefing": "Hybrid work does not reduce productivity; fully remote work may, for narrow task types. A randomized trial found two home days a week had no effect on performance and cut quitting by a third [1]. Two studies of data-entry and call-centre work found fully remote staff 10-20% less productive [2][3]. As always, more research is needed.\n\nSources\n[1] Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT - https://example.org/1\n[2] NBER fully remote data-entry study - https://example.org/2\n[3] Call-centre field experiment - https://example.org/3\n[4] WFH Research survey - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "useful-question-opener", "criterion": "useful", "label": 3, "why": "Borderline: opens by restating the question, but the conclusion arrives in sentence two and nothing is padded. Brent: 3 or 2?", "material": {"topic": "long-term outdoor reliability of perovskite solar cells over ten years or more", "expect": "Says plainly that decade-long field data barely exists, reports what shorter field and accelerated tests show, and does not overstate durability.", "findings": [{"question": "What do accelerated ageing tests show for perovskite modules?", "answer": "Leading lab and pilot modules now pass IEC damp-heat and thermal-cycling tests, with some retaining 80% of output after 1,000-2,000 hours of damp heat.", "sources": ["NREL perovskite durability review", "Joule accelerated ageing study"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How long is the longest outdoor field record?", "answer": "Published outdoor records span about two to three years, with degradation rates that vary widely between groups.", "sources": ["Nature Energy outdoor testing paper"], "confidence": 0.7, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "Do any manufacturers guarantee long lifetimes?", "answer": "One startup announced a 25-year performance warranty for its tandem modules.", "sources": ["Company press release"], "confidence": 0.3, "reviewer_note": "Single press release from the seller; no field data behind the warranty."}], "briefing": "How long do perovskite solar cells last outdoors? Nobody knows yet for ten years or more, because the longest field records cover two to three years [3]. Accelerated tests are encouraging [1][2], and a 25-year warranty from one startup is a marketing claim without field data [4].\n\nSources\n[1] NREL perovskite durability review - https://example.org/1\n[2] Joule accelerated ageing study - https://example.org/2\n[3] Nature Energy outdoor testing paper - https://example.org/3\n[4] Company press release - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "useful-no-conclusion", "criterion": "useful", "label": 1, "why": "A list of facts with no stated answer to what limits deployment.", "material": {"topic": "what limits deployment of small modular reactors in the US", "expect": "Cost and first-of-a-kind risk, NRC licensing, fuel supply (HALEU), and named projects including at least one that was cancelled or delayed.", "findings": [{"question": "What happened to NuScale's first project?", "answer": "NuScale and UAMPS cancelled the Carbon Free Power Project in 2023 after estimated power costs rose to about $89/MWh.", "sources": ["UAMPS announcement", "Utility Dive coverage"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How long does NRC licensing take?", "answer": "Design certification plus a construction permit has historically taken several years; recent reforms set shorter review targets.", "sources": ["NRC licensing overview"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "Is fuel supply a constraint?", "answer": "Many advanced designs need HALEU, and US commercial production is at demonstration scale.", "sources": ["DOE HALEU program page"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "NuScale's Carbon Free Power Project was cancelled in 2023 at about $89/MWh [1][2]. Design certification and a construction permit have taken several years [3]. Many advanced designs need HALEU [4], produced in the US at demonstration scale. The NRC has set shorter review targets [3].\n\nSources\n[1] UAMPS announcement - https://example.org/1\n[2] Utility Dive coverage - https://example.org/2\n[3] NRC licensing overview - https://example.org/3\n[4] DOE HALEU program page - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "covers-history-not-field", "criterion": "covers", "label": 1, "why": "Topic asks for field evidence since 2020; the briefing is mostly history and pre-2020 lab tests.", "material": {"topic": "field performance of air-source heat pumps in cold climates, evidence since 2020 only", "expect": "Field-measured efficiency (COP) at low temperatures, how backup heat is used, and studies from 2020 onward.", "findings": [{"question": "What seasonal efficiency do cold-climate heat pumps reach in the field?", "answer": "Field studies since 2020 in Canada and the northern US report seasonal COPs of about 2.5-3.0 for cold-climate models.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022", "NRCan field monitoring report"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What is the COP near -15 C?", "answer": "One study measured a COP of about 2.0 at -15 C; another measured about 1.5 at the same temperature.", "sources": ["Minnesota CARD field study", "Alaska heat pump monitoring"], "confidence": 0.45, "reviewer_note": "Sources disagree by a third; different models and installs."}, {"question": "How much backup heat is used?", "answer": "Backup resistance or fossil heat supplied 5-20% of seasonal heat in monitored homes, mostly during the coldest days.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Heat pumps were invented in the nineteenth century and spread through Japan and Europe from the 1970s. Early cold-climate models struggled below freezing, and lab tests in the early 2010s found sharp efficiency losses. Today they reach seasonal COPs of 2.5-3.0 [1][2].\n\nSources\n[1] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/1\n[2] NRCan field monitoring report - https://example.org/2\n[3] Minnesota CARD field study - https://example.org/3\n[4] Alaska heat pump monitoring - https://example.org/4\n[5] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/5"}}
{"id": "covers-heatpump-full", "criterion": "covers", "label": 4, "why": "Field COP, low-temperature performance including the disagreement, backup heat, all from 2020 onward.", "material": {"topic": "field performance of air-source heat pumps in cold climates, evidence since 2020 only", "expect": "Field-measured efficiency (COP) at low temperatures, how backup heat is used, and studies from 2020 onward.", "findings": [{"question": "What seasonal efficiency do cold-climate heat pumps reach in the field?", "answer": "Field studies since 2020 in Canada and the northern US report seasonal COPs of about 2.5-3.0 for cold-climate models.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022", "NRCan field monitoring report"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What is the COP near -15 C?", "answer": "One study measured a COP of about 2.0 at -15 C; another measured about 1.5 at the same temperature.", "sources": ["Minnesota CARD field study", "Alaska heat pump monitoring"], "confidence": 0.45, "reviewer_note": "Sources disagree by a third; different models and installs."}, {"question": "How much backup heat is used?", "answer": "Backup resistance or fossil heat supplied 5-20% of seasonal heat in monitored homes, mostly during the coldest days.", "sources": ["NEEP cold-climate field study 2022"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Field studies since 2020 show cold-climate heat pumps reaching seasonal COPs of 2.5-3.0 [1][2]. At -15 C measured COPs range from about 1.5 to 2.0, and the two studies disagree [3][4]. Backup heat supplies 5-20% of seasonal heat, mostly on the coldest days [5].\n\nSources\n[1] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/1\n[2] NRCan field monitoring report - https://example.org/2\n[3] Minnesota CARD field study - https://example.org/3\n[4] Alaska heat pump monitoring - https://example.org/4\n[5] NEEP cold-climate field study 2022 - https://example.org/5"}}
{"id": "covers-postgres-full", "criterion": "covers", "label": 4, "why": "32-bit XIDs, freezing, autovacuum and its setting, and what happens if ignored.", "material": {"topic": "how PostgreSQL prevents transaction ID wraparound", "expect": "32-bit transaction IDs, freezing old tuples, autovacuum and anti-wraparound vacuum, the relevant settings, and what happens if it is ignored.", "findings": [{"question": "Why is wraparound a risk?", "answer": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so after about two billion transactions old rows would appear to be in the future and become invisible.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How does freezing prevent it?", "answer": "VACUUM marks sufficiently old tuples as frozen, removing them from XID comparison; autovacuum forces an anti-wraparound vacuum when a table's relfrozenxid age exceeds autovacuum_freeze_max_age (200 million by default).", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming", "PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What happens if vacuum falls behind?", "answer": "Near the limit PostgreSQL warns, then refuses to assign new transaction IDs until a VACUUM advances the oldest XID; vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work to catch up.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "PostgreSQL prevents wraparound by freezing old rows; XIDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32 [1]. Autovacuum forces a freezing vacuum once a table's relfrozenxid age passes autovacuum_freeze_max_age, 200 million by default [2][3]. If vacuum falls behind, the server stops assigning transaction IDs until it catches up, and vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work [4].\n\nSources\n[1] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/1\n[2] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/2\n[3] PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings - https://example.org/3\n[4] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "covers-postgres-partial", "criterion": "covers", "label": 2, "why": "Explains the cause and freezing but omits the settings and what happens if vacuum falls behind.", "material": {"topic": "how PostgreSQL prevents transaction ID wraparound", "expect": "32-bit transaction IDs, freezing old tuples, autovacuum and anti-wraparound vacuum, the relevant settings, and what happens if it is ignored.", "findings": [{"question": "Why is wraparound a risk?", "answer": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so after about two billion transactions old rows would appear to be in the future and become invisible.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How does freezing prevent it?", "answer": "VACUUM marks sufficiently old tuples as frozen, removing them from XID comparison; autovacuum forces an anti-wraparound vacuum when a table's relfrozenxid age exceeds autovacuum_freeze_max_age (200 million by default).", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming", "PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings"], "confidence": 0.9, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What happens if vacuum falls behind?", "answer": "Near the limit PostgreSQL warns, then refuses to assign new transaction IDs until a VACUUM advances the oldest XID; vacuum_failsafe_age (1.6 billion) makes vacuum skip non-essential work to catch up.", "sources": ["PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit and compared modulo 2^32, so very old rows would look like they are from the future [1]. VACUUM prevents this by freezing old rows [2].\n\nSources\n[1] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/1\n[2] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/2\n[3] PostgreSQL docs: autovacuum settings - https://example.org/3\n[4] PostgreSQL docs: Routine Vacuuming - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "covers-remote-thin", "criterion": "covers", "label": 3, "why": "Distinguishes hybrid from fully remote and avoids a single verdict, but the fully-remote side rests on one sentence.", "material": {"topic": "does remote work reduce employee productivity", "expect": "Shows the evidence is mixed: distinguishes fully remote from hybrid, cites studies on both sides, and does not declare a single verdict.", "findings": [{"question": "What does randomized evidence say about hybrid work?", "answer": "A large randomized trial at Trip.com found two days a week at home had no effect on performance reviews or promotions and cut quitting by a third.", "sources": ["Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What about fully remote work?", "answer": "Two studies of data-entry and call-centre work found fully remote staff 10-20% less productive.", "sources": ["NBER fully remote data-entry study", "Call-centre field experiment"], "confidence": 0.6, "reviewer_note": "Only two studies, both narrow task types; may not generalise to knowledge work."}, {"question": "How do workers rate their own productivity at home?", "answer": "Most surveyed workers say they are as productive or more productive at home.", "sources": ["WFH Research survey"], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "Self-reported; not a productivity measure."}], "briefing": "The answer depends on the arrangement. Hybrid work does not reduce productivity: a large randomized trial found no effect on performance [1]. Fully remote work may cost some productivity [2].\n\nSources\n[1] Nature 2024 hybrid work RCT - https://example.org/1\n[2] NBER fully remote data-entry study - https://example.org/2\n[3] Call-centre field experiment - https://example.org/3\n[4] WFH Research survey - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "covers-smr-thin-licensing", "criterion": "covers", "label": 3, "why": "Cost, a cancelled project and HALEU are covered; licensing gets only a passing mention.", "material": {"topic": "what limits deployment of small modular reactors in the US", "expect": "Cost and first-of-a-kind risk, NRC licensing, fuel supply (HALEU), and named projects including at least one that was cancelled or delayed.", "findings": [{"question": "What happened to NuScale's first project?", "answer": "NuScale and UAMPS cancelled the Carbon Free Power Project in 2023 after estimated power costs rose to about $89/MWh.", "sources": ["UAMPS announcement", "Utility Dive coverage"], "confidence": 0.85, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "How long does NRC licensing take?", "answer": "Design certification plus a construction permit has historically taken several years; recent reforms set shorter review targets.", "sources": ["NRC licensing overview"], "confidence": 0.75, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "Is fuel supply a constraint?", "answer": "Many advanced designs need HALEU, and US commercial production is at demonstration scale.", "sources": ["DOE HALEU program page"], "confidence": 0.8, "reviewer_note": ""}], "briefing": "Cost is the main limit: NuScale's first project was cancelled in 2023 when power costs rose to about $89/MWh [1][2]. Fuel is the next: many designs need HALEU, produced in the US only at demonstration scale [4]. Licensing also takes time [3].\n\nSources\n[1] UAMPS announcement - https://example.org/1\n[2] Utility Dive coverage - https://example.org/2\n[3] NRC licensing overview - https://example.org/3\n[4] DOE HALEU program page - https://example.org/4"}}
{"id": "plan-heatpump-good", "criterion": "plan_quality", "label": 4, "why": "Distinct, answerable, covers efficiency, cold-snap performance and backup heat, and every question respects 'since 2020'.", "material": {"topic": "field performance of air-source heat pumps in cold climates, evidence since 2020 only", "plan": ["What seasonal COPs have field studies since 2020 measured for cold-climate heat pumps?", "How do they perform below -15 C in field data from 2020 onward?", "How much backup heat did monitored homes use since 2020?", "Which installation factors explain differences between field studies?"], "rationale": ["Headline efficiency.", "The cold-snap question readers care about.", "Shows real-world reliance on resistance or fossil heat.", "Explains spread in results."]}}
