{"id": "grounded-q-fuel-real", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 3, "why": "Real briefing text. Every claim is in finding 1, but both sentences cite [8], whose only quote is the ~900 kg/yr figure; the Centrus licence, the 2029 date and TerraPower's slip are quoted from [7]. Right finding, less specific source.", "material": {"topic": "what limits deployment of small modular reactors in the US", "expect": "Cost and first-of-a-kind risk, NRC licensing, fuel supply (HALEU), and named projects including at least one that was cancelled or delayed.", "findings": [{"question": "What are the cost, supply chain, and manufacturing challenges (e.g., HALEU fuel availability, forging capacity, first-of-a-kind cost overruns) currently constraining SMR projects in the US?", "answer": "**Fuel is the binding constraint, and HALEU is the main one.** Centrus, the only NRC-licensed HALEU producer, finished its demonstration production under a DOE contract in June 2026 and is targeting 12 metric tons of HALEU per year from new cascades starting in 2029. Against that, one industry tally puts current US production at about 900 kg/yr, and DOE has said HALEU shortages threaten timelines for NuScale, Oklo, TerraPower, and X-energy. TerraPower's Natrium shows the cost: its commercial-operation target moved from 2028 to 2030-31 primarily because of fuel availability.\n\n**Forging and manufacturing capacity is a weaker, less-documented constraint.** One analysis reports acute shortages in skilled labor and domestic forging capacity, but it is a secondary source and I found no primary data on forging throughput. It also describes the supply chain as unwilling to invest in new factories without firm orders, which is a demand-side problem rather than a fixed capacity limit.\n\n**First-of-a-kind cost overruns are the central economic risk, and the numbers conflict.** NuScale's Utah project saw its target power price climb from $55 per megawatt-hour to $89 before utilities withdrew in 2023. Published construction-cost estimates range from about $4,000 to $18,000 per kilowatt, and an IEEFA-cited TVA filing implies an estimated overnight cost for the BWRX-300 of $17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars.\n\n**The first US project's cost remains unsettled.** Earlier reporting put the first Clinch River unit at about $5.4 billion before interest and tax credits, but after the NRC's construction permit, coverage says TVA has not published an official cost estimate for Clinch River. TVA also said it continues negotiations with industry partners to ensure responsible cost sharing, and no construction start date has been announced.\n\n**Federal money is targeting these gaps, but it is not yet proven.** DOE's January 2026 announcement of $2.7 billion in HALEU and LEU enrichment task orders is aimed at fuel supply, and DOE separately is backing TVA with up to $400 million in cost-shared funding. Most of the evidence above comes from advocacy-oriented or secondary sources, so the timelines and cost figures should be treated as provisional.", "sources": [{"n": 7, "title": "BASELOAD SPECIAL: SMALL MODULAR REACTORS -- Safety, Hazards, Applications", "quotes": ["## Fuel supply Centrus Energy, the only NRC-licensed HALEU producer, finished its demonstration production under a DOE contract in June 2026 and is ta...", "The commercial-operation target moved from 2028 to 2030-31 primarily because of fuel availability.", "NuScale’s Utah project saw its target power price climb from $55 per megawatt-hour to $89 before members of the Utah Associated Municipal Power System..."]}, {"n": 8, "title": "State of Small Modular Reactors 2026 — Annual Intelligence Report", "quotes": ["Current US Production ~900 kg/yr"]}, {"n": 9, "title": "Financing the Nuclear Renaissance, Part II : How SMRs Will Actually Be Paid For - Oil & Gas 360", "quotes": ["**Designs dependent on HALEU with no diversified supply route** When DOE itself says HALEU shortages “threaten timelines for NuScale, Oklo, TerraPowe...", "**The HALEU Funding Push: Billions To Solve One Problem** DOE’s January 2026 announcement — awarding $2.7 billion in HALEU and LEU enrichment task or..."]}, {"n": 10, "title": "X-energy Nuclear 2026, $700M for SMRs, Amazon Deal - ENKI", "quotes": ["| Acute shortages in skilled labor and domestic forging capacity are exposed.", "This has created a \\\"chicken-and-egg\\\" problem where suppliers are unwilling to invest in new factories"]}, {"n": 11, "title": "Rush to Build New Nuclear Power: TVA and Administration Ignore Cost and Safety - Southern Alliance for Clean Energy (SACE)", "quotes": ["According to Schlissel’s testimony, TVA’s recent filings reveal an estimated overnight cost for the BWRX-300 of $17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars. ..."]}, {"n": 12, "title": "Tennessee Valley Authority Advances Plans to Build a Small Modular Reactor at Clinch River Site", "quotes": ["- The first unit could come online around 2032, add 300 megawatts of electricity, and cost about $5.4 billion before interest and tax credits."]}, {"n": 13, "title": "Green Light for America's First Small Modular Reactor", "quotes": ["TVA has not published an official cost estimate for Clinch River."]}, {"n": 14, "title": "NRC Construction Permit to TVA for BWRX-300", "quotes": ["The Tennessee Valley Authority (TVA) achieved a nationally significant milestone in America’s nuclear energy future with the U.S. ... ## NRC Construc..."]}], "confidence": 0.5, "reviewer_note": "Cost-per-kW estimates range implausibly widely ($4,000-$18,000/kW) with no reconciliation; forging capacity claim rests on a single secondary source with no primary data; overall framed as provisional by the answer itself."}, {"question": "Why did Bechtel cease involvement with TerraPower's Natrium project, and how does this affect the project's cost or schedule?", "answer": "Bechtel and TerraPower said they could not agree on the next phase of the Natrium project, so they decided to proceed separately, but neither has explained the disagreement in detail. The split was disclosed in public filings in Virginia, after the companies failed to agree on the next stages. Bechtel's work so far was done under an earlier phase of its agreement with TerraPower, before the project entered full EPC scope. Bechtel will stay on to finish the Sodium Test and Fill Facility, and TerraPower said it will rebid the engineering, procurement and construction contract for the estimated $4-billion project. On cost, the only figure reported is that $4 billion estimate, which dates to the Associated Press reporting in 2024 that the cost to build Natrium would be up to $4 billion. One trade publication warns that Bechtel's departure raises hard questions about Natrium's 2030 timeline and whether costs will balloon as TerraPower hunts for a replacement contractor, but that is analysis rather than a reported overrun, and I found no new cost estimate.", "sources": [{"n": 32, "title": "TerraPower, Bechtel part ways on the Kemmerer Natrium project - ExchangeMonitor", "quotes": ["after failing to come to an agreement on the next stages, according to public disclosure papers filed in Virginia."]}, {"n": 33, "title": "TerraPower Reopens EPC Competition for $4B Natrium Nuclear Project", "quotes": ["Bechtel spokeswoman Molly Edwards said the contractor's work to date was performed under an earlier phase of its agreement with TerraPower, before the...", "# TerraPower Reopens EPC Competition for $4B Natrium Nuclear Project ## Longtime contractor Bechtel exits full phase work five months after nuclear co..."]}, {"n": 34, "title": "Bechtel breaks ground on $4B Bill Gates-backed nuclear reactor", "quotes": ["- The Associated Press reported in 2024 that the cost to build Natrium would be up to $4 billion."]}, {"n": 35, "title": "Bechtel Exits TerraPower, Holtec Delays IPO: Nuclear Sector", "quotes": ["Bechtel's departure raises hard questions about Natrium's 2030 timeline and whether costs will balloon as TerraPower hunts for a replacement contracto..."]}], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "The core causal question (why) is explicitly unanswered by the sources; cost figure cited ($4B) predates the split and schedule impact is speculative analysis, not reported fact."}], "briefing": "Fuel supply is the binding physical constraint. The US has only one NRC-licensed HALEU producer (Centrus), current output is roughly 900 kg/year against the gigawatt-scale needs of designs from TerraPower, X-energy, Oklo and NuScale, and meaningful capacity (12 metric tons/year) isn't expected until 2029 [8]. TerraPower's own commercial-operation target slipped from 2028 to 2030-31 largely because of fuel availability [8]."}}
{"id": "grounded-q-fuel-fixed", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 4, "why": "The real paragraph with citations corrected to [7][8] and [7]: every claim is in finding 1 and each cited source's quotes carry it.", "material": {"topic": "what limits deployment of small modular reactors in the US", "expect": "Cost and first-of-a-kind risk, NRC licensing, fuel supply (HALEU), and named projects including at least one that was cancelled or delayed.", "findings": [{"question": "What are the cost, supply chain, and manufacturing challenges (e.g., HALEU fuel availability, forging capacity, first-of-a-kind cost overruns) currently constraining SMR projects in the US?", "answer": "**Fuel is the binding constraint, and HALEU is the main one.** Centrus, the only NRC-licensed HALEU producer, finished its demonstration production under a DOE contract in June 2026 and is targeting 12 metric tons of HALEU per year from new cascades starting in 2029. Against that, one industry tally puts current US production at about 900 kg/yr, and DOE has said HALEU shortages threaten timelines for NuScale, Oklo, TerraPower, and X-energy. TerraPower's Natrium shows the cost: its commercial-operation target moved from 2028 to 2030-31 primarily because of fuel availability.\n\n**Forging and manufacturing capacity is a weaker, less-documented constraint.** One analysis reports acute shortages in skilled labor and domestic forging capacity, but it is a secondary source and I found no primary data on forging throughput. It also describes the supply chain as unwilling to invest in new factories without firm orders, which is a demand-side problem rather than a fixed capacity limit.\n\n**First-of-a-kind cost overruns are the central economic risk, and the numbers conflict.** NuScale's Utah project saw its target power price climb from $55 per megawatt-hour to $89 before utilities withdrew in 2023. Published construction-cost estimates range from about $4,000 to $18,000 per kilowatt, and an IEEFA-cited TVA filing implies an estimated overnight cost for the BWRX-300 of $17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars.\n\n**The first US project's cost remains unsettled.** Earlier reporting put the first Clinch River unit at about $5.4 billion before interest and tax credits, but after the NRC's construction permit, coverage says TVA has not published an official cost estimate for Clinch River. TVA also said it continues negotiations with industry partners to ensure responsible cost sharing, and no construction start date has been announced.\n\n**Federal money is targeting these gaps, but it is not yet proven.** DOE's January 2026 announcement of $2.7 billion in HALEU and LEU enrichment task orders is aimed at fuel supply, and DOE separately is backing TVA with up to $400 million in cost-shared funding. Most of the evidence above comes from advocacy-oriented or secondary sources, so the timelines and cost figures should be treated as provisional.", "sources": [{"n": 7, "title": "BASELOAD SPECIAL: SMALL MODULAR REACTORS -- Safety, Hazards, Applications", "quotes": ["## Fuel supply Centrus Energy, the only NRC-licensed HALEU producer, finished its demonstration production under a DOE contract in June 2026 and is ta...", "The commercial-operation target moved from 2028 to 2030-31 primarily because of fuel availability.", "NuScale’s Utah project saw its target power price climb from $55 per megawatt-hour to $89 before members of the Utah Associated Municipal Power System..."]}, {"n": 8, "title": "State of Small Modular Reactors 2026 — Annual Intelligence Report", "quotes": ["Current US Production ~900 kg/yr"]}, {"n": 9, "title": "Financing the Nuclear Renaissance, Part II : How SMRs Will Actually Be Paid For - Oil & Gas 360", "quotes": ["**Designs dependent on HALEU with no diversified supply route** When DOE itself says HALEU shortages “threaten timelines for NuScale, Oklo, TerraPowe...", "**The HALEU Funding Push: Billions To Solve One Problem** DOE’s January 2026 announcement — awarding $2.7 billion in HALEU and LEU enrichment task or..."]}, {"n": 10, "title": "X-energy Nuclear 2026, $700M for SMRs, Amazon Deal - ENKI", "quotes": ["| Acute shortages in skilled labor and domestic forging capacity are exposed.", "This has created a \\\"chicken-and-egg\\\" problem where suppliers are unwilling to invest in new factories"]}, {"n": 11, "title": "Rush to Build New Nuclear Power: TVA and Administration Ignore Cost and Safety - Southern Alliance for Clean Energy (SACE)", "quotes": ["According to Schlissel’s testimony, TVA’s recent filings reveal an estimated overnight cost for the BWRX-300 of $17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars. ..."]}, {"n": 12, "title": "Tennessee Valley Authority Advances Plans to Build a Small Modular Reactor at Clinch River Site", "quotes": ["- The first unit could come online around 2032, add 300 megawatts of electricity, and cost about $5.4 billion before interest and tax credits."]}, {"n": 13, "title": "Green Light for America's First Small Modular Reactor", "quotes": ["TVA has not published an official cost estimate for Clinch River."]}, {"n": 14, "title": "NRC Construction Permit to TVA for BWRX-300", "quotes": ["The Tennessee Valley Authority (TVA) achieved a nationally significant milestone in America’s nuclear energy future with the U.S. ... ## NRC Construc..."]}], "confidence": 0.5, "reviewer_note": "Cost-per-kW estimates range implausibly widely ($4,000-$18,000/kW) with no reconciliation; forging capacity claim rests on a single secondary source with no primary data; overall framed as provisional by the answer itself."}, {"question": "Why did Bechtel cease involvement with TerraPower's Natrium project, and how does this affect the project's cost or schedule?", "answer": "Bechtel and TerraPower said they could not agree on the next phase of the Natrium project, so they decided to proceed separately, but neither has explained the disagreement in detail. The split was disclosed in public filings in Virginia, after the companies failed to agree on the next stages. Bechtel's work so far was done under an earlier phase of its agreement with TerraPower, before the project entered full EPC scope. Bechtel will stay on to finish the Sodium Test and Fill Facility, and TerraPower said it will rebid the engineering, procurement and construction contract for the estimated $4-billion project. On cost, the only figure reported is that $4 billion estimate, which dates to the Associated Press reporting in 2024 that the cost to build Natrium would be up to $4 billion. One trade publication warns that Bechtel's departure raises hard questions about Natrium's 2030 timeline and whether costs will balloon as TerraPower hunts for a replacement contractor, but that is analysis rather than a reported overrun, and I found no new cost estimate.", "sources": [{"n": 32, "title": "TerraPower, Bechtel part ways on the Kemmerer Natrium project - ExchangeMonitor", "quotes": ["after failing to come to an agreement on the next stages, according to public disclosure papers filed in Virginia."]}, {"n": 33, "title": "TerraPower Reopens EPC Competition for $4B Natrium Nuclear Project", "quotes": ["Bechtel spokeswoman Molly Edwards said the contractor's work to date was performed under an earlier phase of its agreement with TerraPower, before the...", "# TerraPower Reopens EPC Competition for $4B Natrium Nuclear Project ## Longtime contractor Bechtel exits full phase work five months after nuclear co..."]}, {"n": 34, "title": "Bechtel breaks ground on $4B Bill Gates-backed nuclear reactor", "quotes": ["- The Associated Press reported in 2024 that the cost to build Natrium would be up to $4 billion."]}, {"n": 35, "title": "Bechtel Exits TerraPower, Holtec Delays IPO: Nuclear Sector", "quotes": ["Bechtel's departure raises hard questions about Natrium's 2030 timeline and whether costs will balloon as TerraPower hunts for a replacement contracto..."]}], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "The core causal question (why) is explicitly unanswered by the sources; cost figure cited ($4B) predates the split and schedule impact is speculative analysis, not reported fact."}], "briefing": "Fuel supply is the binding physical constraint. The US has only one NRC-licensed HALEU producer (Centrus), current output is roughly 900 kg/year against the gigawatt-scale needs of designs from TerraPower, X-energy, Oklo and NuScale, and meaningful capacity (12 metric tons/year) isn't expected until 2029 [7][8]. TerraPower's own commercial-operation target slipped from 2028 to 2030-31 largely because of fuel availability [7]."}}
{"id": "grounded-q-fuel-invented", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 2, "why": "Corrected paragraph plus one invented sentence: the finding says 2029, and [9] is about DOE funding, not a Centrus date. One clear unsupported claim.", "material": {"topic": "what limits deployment of small modular reactors in the US", "expect": "Cost and first-of-a-kind risk, NRC licensing, fuel supply (HALEU), and named projects including at least one that was cancelled or delayed.", "findings": [{"question": "What are the cost, supply chain, and manufacturing challenges (e.g., HALEU fuel availability, forging capacity, first-of-a-kind cost overruns) currently constraining SMR projects in the US?", "answer": "**Fuel is the binding constraint, and HALEU is the main one.** Centrus, the only NRC-licensed HALEU producer, finished its demonstration production under a DOE contract in June 2026 and is targeting 12 metric tons of HALEU per year from new cascades starting in 2029. Against that, one industry tally puts current US production at about 900 kg/yr, and DOE has said HALEU shortages threaten timelines for NuScale, Oklo, TerraPower, and X-energy. TerraPower's Natrium shows the cost: its commercial-operation target moved from 2028 to 2030-31 primarily because of fuel availability.\n\n**Forging and manufacturing capacity is a weaker, less-documented constraint.** One analysis reports acute shortages in skilled labor and domestic forging capacity, but it is a secondary source and I found no primary data on forging throughput. It also describes the supply chain as unwilling to invest in new factories without firm orders, which is a demand-side problem rather than a fixed capacity limit.\n\n**First-of-a-kind cost overruns are the central economic risk, and the numbers conflict.** NuScale's Utah project saw its target power price climb from $55 per megawatt-hour to $89 before utilities withdrew in 2023. Published construction-cost estimates range from about $4,000 to $18,000 per kilowatt, and an IEEFA-cited TVA filing implies an estimated overnight cost for the BWRX-300 of $17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars.\n\n**The first US project's cost remains unsettled.** Earlier reporting put the first Clinch River unit at about $5.4 billion before interest and tax credits, but after the NRC's construction permit, coverage says TVA has not published an official cost estimate for Clinch River. TVA also said it continues negotiations with industry partners to ensure responsible cost sharing, and no construction start date has been announced.\n\n**Federal money is targeting these gaps, but it is not yet proven.** DOE's January 2026 announcement of $2.7 billion in HALEU and LEU enrichment task orders is aimed at fuel supply, and DOE separately is backing TVA with up to $400 million in cost-shared funding. Most of the evidence above comes from advocacy-oriented or secondary sources, so the timelines and cost figures should be treated as provisional.", "sources": [{"n": 7, "title": "BASELOAD SPECIAL: SMALL MODULAR REACTORS -- Safety, Hazards, Applications", "quotes": ["## Fuel supply Centrus Energy, the only NRC-licensed HALEU producer, finished its demonstration production under a DOE contract in June 2026 and is ta...", "The commercial-operation target moved from 2028 to 2030-31 primarily because of fuel availability.", "NuScale’s Utah project saw its target power price climb from $55 per megawatt-hour to $89 before members of the Utah Associated Municipal Power System..."]}, {"n": 8, "title": "State of Small Modular Reactors 2026 — Annual Intelligence Report", "quotes": ["Current US Production ~900 kg/yr"]}, {"n": 9, "title": "Financing the Nuclear Renaissance, Part II : How SMRs Will Actually Be Paid For - Oil & Gas 360", "quotes": ["**Designs dependent on HALEU with no diversified supply route** When DOE itself says HALEU shortages “threaten timelines for NuScale, Oklo, TerraPowe...", "**The HALEU Funding Push: Billions To Solve One Problem** DOE’s January 2026 announcement — awarding $2.7 billion in HALEU and LEU enrichment task or..."]}, {"n": 10, "title": "X-energy Nuclear 2026, $700M for SMRs, Amazon Deal - ENKI", "quotes": ["| Acute shortages in skilled labor and domestic forging capacity are exposed.", "This has created a \\\"chicken-and-egg\\\" problem where suppliers are unwilling to invest in new factories"]}, {"n": 11, "title": "Rush to Build New Nuclear Power: TVA and Administration Ignore Cost and Safety - Southern Alliance for Clean Energy (SACE)", "quotes": ["According to Schlissel’s testimony, TVA’s recent filings reveal an estimated overnight cost for the BWRX-300 of $17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars. ..."]}, {"n": 12, "title": "Tennessee Valley Authority Advances Plans to Build a Small Modular Reactor at Clinch River Site", "quotes": ["- The first unit could come online around 2032, add 300 megawatts of electricity, and cost about $5.4 billion before interest and tax credits."]}, {"n": 13, "title": "Green Light for America's First Small Modular Reactor", "quotes": ["TVA has not published an official cost estimate for Clinch River."]}, {"n": 14, "title": "NRC Construction Permit to TVA for BWRX-300", "quotes": ["The Tennessee Valley Authority (TVA) achieved a nationally significant milestone in America’s nuclear energy future with the U.S. ... ## NRC Construc..."]}], "confidence": 0.5, "reviewer_note": "Cost-per-kW estimates range implausibly widely ($4,000-$18,000/kW) with no reconciliation; forging capacity claim rests on a single secondary source with no primary data; overall framed as provisional by the answer itself."}, {"question": "Why did Bechtel cease involvement with TerraPower's Natrium project, and how does this affect the project's cost or schedule?", "answer": "Bechtel and TerraPower said they could not agree on the next phase of the Natrium project, so they decided to proceed separately, but neither has explained the disagreement in detail. The split was disclosed in public filings in Virginia, after the companies failed to agree on the next stages. Bechtel's work so far was done under an earlier phase of its agreement with TerraPower, before the project entered full EPC scope. Bechtel will stay on to finish the Sodium Test and Fill Facility, and TerraPower said it will rebid the engineering, procurement and construction contract for the estimated $4-billion project. On cost, the only figure reported is that $4 billion estimate, which dates to the Associated Press reporting in 2024 that the cost to build Natrium would be up to $4 billion. One trade publication warns that Bechtel's departure raises hard questions about Natrium's 2030 timeline and whether costs will balloon as TerraPower hunts for a replacement contractor, but that is analysis rather than a reported overrun, and I found no new cost estimate.", "sources": [{"n": 32, "title": "TerraPower, Bechtel part ways on the Kemmerer Natrium project - ExchangeMonitor", "quotes": ["after failing to come to an agreement on the next stages, according to public disclosure papers filed in Virginia."]}, {"n": 33, "title": "TerraPower Reopens EPC Competition for $4B Natrium Nuclear Project", "quotes": ["Bechtel spokeswoman Molly Edwards said the contractor's work to date was performed under an earlier phase of its agreement with TerraPower, before the...", "# TerraPower Reopens EPC Competition for $4B Natrium Nuclear Project ## Longtime contractor Bechtel exits full phase work five months after nuclear co..."]}, {"n": 34, "title": "Bechtel breaks ground on $4B Bill Gates-backed nuclear reactor", "quotes": ["- The Associated Press reported in 2024 that the cost to build Natrium would be up to $4 billion."]}, {"n": 35, "title": "Bechtel Exits TerraPower, Holtec Delays IPO: Nuclear Sector", "quotes": ["Bechtel's departure raises hard questions about Natrium's 2030 timeline and whether costs will balloon as TerraPower hunts for a replacement contracto..."]}], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "The core causal question (why) is explicitly unanswered by the sources; cost figure cited ($4B) predates the split and schedule impact is speculative analysis, not reported fact."}], "briefing": "Fuel supply is the binding physical constraint. The US has only one NRC-licensed HALEU producer (Centrus), current output is roughly 900 kg/year against the gigawatt-scale needs of designs from TerraPower, X-energy, Oklo and NuScale, and meaningful capacity (12 metric tons/year) isn't expected until 2029 [7][8]. TerraPower's own commercial-operation target slipped from 2028 to 2030-31 largely because of fuel availability [7]. DOE now expects Centrus to reach the full 12-tonne output by 2027 [9]."}}
{"id": "grounded-q-aioffice-real", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 4, "why": "Real briefing text. Powers and the absence of reported enforcement are both in finding 4; [18][19][20] quote the powers, and the 'no fine reported' caveat is the finding's own.", "material": {"topic": "obligations for general-purpose AI model providers under EU law since 2024 (EU only, not US or UK)", "expect": "The AI Act's general-purpose AI model obligations, their timeline, the systemic-risk tier, and the code of practice. Stays within the EU as asked.", "findings": [{"question": "What enforcement mechanisms, penalties, and institutional roles (AI Office, national authorities, Board) apply to GPAI providers for non-compliance, and have there been any enforcement actions or market developments (e.g., provider withdrawals, compliance disputes) since 2024?", "answer": "The AI Office runs GPAI enforcement directly, with powers to request technical documentation, run evaluations, demand corrective steps and issue fines, while national competent authorities take on other AI systems operating within their borders. Fines reach the higher of €15 million or 3 percent of the provider's worldwide annual turnover, and the Commission can also request a provider to restrict the making available on the market, withdraw or recall the model. The obligations applied from 2 August 2025, but the enforcement powers only became usable on 2 August 2026, and the AI Office has said it may use these powers in cases where technical compliance dialogues are not sufficient. I found no reported fine, formal investigation, or withdrawal order against a GPAI provider as of early October 2026, though my searches were limited and this rests on law-firm and commentary sources rather than an official enforcement register. On the market side, Meta said it won't be signing the Code, calling it introducing \"a number of legal uncertainties for model developers\", while OpenAI and Mistral have already signed the code. Sources disagree on signatory counts (one says more than 180 companies, another says roughly two dozen total signatories), and I could not find a source describing the AI Board's specific enforcement role.", "sources": [{"n": 18, "title": "EU begins enforcing AI Act, putting AI models under the microscope - Help Net Security", "quotes": ["The AI Office handles general-purpose AI models directly, with power to request technical documentation, run evaluations, demand corrective steps and ...", "National competent authorities take on other AI systems operating within their borders."]}, {"n": 19, "title": "EU AI Act Enforcement Phase Begins", "quotes": ["These powers are significant and include requesting information and documentation, obtaining access to models for evaluation, requiring corrective or ...", "The substantive obligations for providers of GPAI models placed on the market on or after August 2, 2025, have been applicable since that date, althou..."]}, {"n": 20, "title": "Frequently Asked Questions", "quotes": ["The powers include requesting information, requesting access to a model for evaluations, requiring risk mitigation measures, and issuing fines of up t...", "**The AI Office may use these powers in cases where technical compliance dialogues are not sufficient."]}, {"n": 21, "title": "Meta declines to sign EU AI code of practice", "quotes": ["We have carefully reviewed the European Commission's Code of Practice for general-purpose AI (GPAI) models and Meta won't be signing it,\" Kaplan said ...", "OpenAI and Mistral have already signed the code."]}, {"n": 22, "title": "Meta Skipped the EU AI Code: What Llama Dep… · AI Policy Desk", "quotes": ["More than 180 companies signed the Code of Practice to earn presumptive compliance."]}, {"n": 23, "title": "The EU AI Act GPAI Code of Practice: What It Is and ...", "quotes": ["As of this writing, signatories include major general-purpose model providers **Amazon, Anthropic, Google, IBM, Microsoft, Mistral AI, and OpenAI**, a..."]}], "confidence": 0.5, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What are the key dates and transitional timelines for GPAI obligations under the EU AI Act, including entry into force, the August 2025 application date for GPAI rules, and grandfathering provisions for existing models?", "answer": "The AI Act entered into force on 1 August 2024, and its general-purpose AI (GPAI) chapter began to apply on 2 August 2025, with Chapter V, the governance chapters, and the Chapter XII penalty framework taking effect that day. Models placed on the market from 2 August 2025 onward have to comply immediately. Models already on the market before 2 August 2025 must ensure compliance by 2 August 2027. The Commission could not enforce these duties until 2 August 2026, when its fining powers also became active, with fines of up to the greater of either €15 million or 3% of a company's global annual turnover. Sources conflict on fine timing for legacy models: Jones Day reads the rules as fines applying from 2 August 2026 for models where both original and modified versions were placed on the EU market after 2 August 2025, and from 2 August 2027 where both unmodified and modified versions were on the market on or before 2 August 2025, and it says it is unclear which regime covers legacy models modified after August 2025. Separately, the Commission's guidelines say legacy models need not be retrained or \"unlearned\" where that is impossible or disproportionate, but providers must disclose and justify this in their copyright policy and training-content summary.", "sources": [{"n": 4, "title": "EU AI Act: what applies from 2 August 2026 (GPAI enforcement + Article 50)", "quotes": ["| Article 113(a) | | 2 August 2025 | Chapter V (general-purpose AI models), governance chapters, and the Chapter XII penalty framework apply."]}, {"n": 5, "title": "EU rules on general-purpose AI models start to apply, bringing more transparency, safety and accountability", "quotes": ["Models already on the market before 2 August 2025 must ensure compliance by 2 August 2027."]}, {"n": 6, "title": "EU AI Act News: Rules on General-Purpose AI Start Applying, Guidelines and Template for Summary of Training Data Finalized", "quotes": ["The Commission can only take enforcement action from 2 August 2026, but this does not affect the applicability of obligations from 2 August 2025."]}, {"n": 7, "title": "EU AI Act: European Commission Publishes General-Purpose AI Code of Practice", "quotes": ["As from 2 August 2026, failure to satisfy the AI Act's requirements for GPAI models may result in substantial penalties, including the greater of eith...", "These fines will apply: From 2 August 2026, for GPAI models where both original and modified versions were placed on the EU market after 2 August 2025..."]}], "confidence": 0.72, "reviewer_note": "Explicitly flags an unresolved conflict (Jones Day) on which fine-timing regime applies to modified legacy models, without resolving it."}], "briefing": "On enforcement, the AI Office (not national authorities) directly oversees GPAI compliance, with power to demand documentation, run evaluations, and impose fines or market withdrawal [18][19][20]. As of early October 2026, no fine, investigation, or withdrawal order against a GPAI provider has been reported, though this is based on limited commentary rather than an official register [18][19]."}}
{"id": "grounded-q-aioffice-wrongcite", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 2, "why": "Real paragraph with the second sentence's citation changed to [21]: same finding, but [21] is Meta declining the Code, nothing about enforcement. A citation to the wrong source.", "material": {"topic": "obligations for general-purpose AI model providers under EU law since 2024 (EU only, not US or UK)", "expect": "The AI Act's general-purpose AI model obligations, their timeline, the systemic-risk tier, and the code of practice. Stays within the EU as asked.", "findings": [{"question": "What enforcement mechanisms, penalties, and institutional roles (AI Office, national authorities, Board) apply to GPAI providers for non-compliance, and have there been any enforcement actions or market developments (e.g., provider withdrawals, compliance disputes) since 2024?", "answer": "The AI Office runs GPAI enforcement directly, with powers to request technical documentation, run evaluations, demand corrective steps and issue fines, while national competent authorities take on other AI systems operating within their borders. Fines reach the higher of €15 million or 3 percent of the provider's worldwide annual turnover, and the Commission can also request a provider to restrict the making available on the market, withdraw or recall the model. The obligations applied from 2 August 2025, but the enforcement powers only became usable on 2 August 2026, and the AI Office has said it may use these powers in cases where technical compliance dialogues are not sufficient. I found no reported fine, formal investigation, or withdrawal order against a GPAI provider as of early October 2026, though my searches were limited and this rests on law-firm and commentary sources rather than an official enforcement register. On the market side, Meta said it won't be signing the Code, calling it introducing \"a number of legal uncertainties for model developers\", while OpenAI and Mistral have already signed the code. Sources disagree on signatory counts (one says more than 180 companies, another says roughly two dozen total signatories), and I could not find a source describing the AI Board's specific enforcement role.", "sources": [{"n": 18, "title": "EU begins enforcing AI Act, putting AI models under the microscope - Help Net Security", "quotes": ["The AI Office handles general-purpose AI models directly, with power to request technical documentation, run evaluations, demand corrective steps and ...", "National competent authorities take on other AI systems operating within their borders."]}, {"n": 19, "title": "EU AI Act Enforcement Phase Begins", "quotes": ["These powers are significant and include requesting information and documentation, obtaining access to models for evaluation, requiring corrective or ...", "The substantive obligations for providers of GPAI models placed on the market on or after August 2, 2025, have been applicable since that date, althou..."]}, {"n": 20, "title": "Frequently Asked Questions", "quotes": ["The powers include requesting information, requesting access to a model for evaluations, requiring risk mitigation measures, and issuing fines of up t...", "**The AI Office may use these powers in cases where technical compliance dialogues are not sufficient."]}, {"n": 21, "title": "Meta declines to sign EU AI code of practice", "quotes": ["We have carefully reviewed the European Commission's Code of Practice for general-purpose AI (GPAI) models and Meta won't be signing it,\" Kaplan said ...", "OpenAI and Mistral have already signed the code."]}, {"n": 22, "title": "Meta Skipped the EU AI Code: What Llama Dep… · AI Policy Desk", "quotes": ["More than 180 companies signed the Code of Practice to earn presumptive compliance."]}, {"n": 23, "title": "The EU AI Act GPAI Code of Practice: What It Is and ...", "quotes": ["As of this writing, signatories include major general-purpose model providers **Amazon, Anthropic, Google, IBM, Microsoft, Mistral AI, and OpenAI**, a..."]}], "confidence": 0.5, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What are the key dates and transitional timelines for GPAI obligations under the EU AI Act, including entry into force, the August 2025 application date for GPAI rules, and grandfathering provisions for existing models?", "answer": "The AI Act entered into force on 1 August 2024, and its general-purpose AI (GPAI) chapter began to apply on 2 August 2025, with Chapter V, the governance chapters, and the Chapter XII penalty framework taking effect that day. Models placed on the market from 2 August 2025 onward have to comply immediately. Models already on the market before 2 August 2025 must ensure compliance by 2 August 2027. The Commission could not enforce these duties until 2 August 2026, when its fining powers also became active, with fines of up to the greater of either €15 million or 3% of a company's global annual turnover. Sources conflict on fine timing for legacy models: Jones Day reads the rules as fines applying from 2 August 2026 for models where both original and modified versions were placed on the EU market after 2 August 2025, and from 2 August 2027 where both unmodified and modified versions were on the market on or before 2 August 2025, and it says it is unclear which regime covers legacy models modified after August 2025. Separately, the Commission's guidelines say legacy models need not be retrained or \"unlearned\" where that is impossible or disproportionate, but providers must disclose and justify this in their copyright policy and training-content summary.", "sources": [{"n": 4, "title": "EU AI Act: what applies from 2 August 2026 (GPAI enforcement + Article 50)", "quotes": ["| Article 113(a) | | 2 August 2025 | Chapter V (general-purpose AI models), governance chapters, and the Chapter XII penalty framework apply."]}, {"n": 5, "title": "EU rules on general-purpose AI models start to apply, bringing more transparency, safety and accountability", "quotes": ["Models already on the market before 2 August 2025 must ensure compliance by 2 August 2027."]}, {"n": 6, "title": "EU AI Act News: Rules on General-Purpose AI Start Applying, Guidelines and Template for Summary of Training Data Finalized", "quotes": ["The Commission can only take enforcement action from 2 August 2026, but this does not affect the applicability of obligations from 2 August 2025."]}, {"n": 7, "title": "EU AI Act: European Commission Publishes General-Purpose AI Code of Practice", "quotes": ["As from 2 August 2026, failure to satisfy the AI Act's requirements for GPAI models may result in substantial penalties, including the greater of eith...", "These fines will apply: From 2 August 2026, for GPAI models where both original and modified versions were placed on the EU market after 2 August 2025..."]}], "confidence": 0.72, "reviewer_note": "Explicitly flags an unresolved conflict (Jones Day) on which fine-timing regime applies to modified legacy models, without resolving it."}], "briefing": "On enforcement, the AI Office (not national authorities) directly oversees GPAI compliance, with power to demand documentation, run evaluations, and impose fines or market withdrawal [18][19][20]. As of early October 2026, no fine, investigation, or withdrawal order against a GPAI provider has been reported, though this is based on limited commentary rather than an official register [21]."}}
{"id": "grounded-q-aioffice-invented", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 1, "why": "Two unsupported claims: the finding gives 3% (not 7%), and it says no investigation has been reported; [19] supports neither. Several unsupported claims.", "material": {"topic": "obligations for general-purpose AI model providers under EU law since 2024 (EU only, not US or UK)", "expect": "The AI Act's general-purpose AI model obligations, their timeline, the systemic-risk tier, and the code of practice. Stays within the EU as asked.", "findings": [{"question": "What enforcement mechanisms, penalties, and institutional roles (AI Office, national authorities, Board) apply to GPAI providers for non-compliance, and have there been any enforcement actions or market developments (e.g., provider withdrawals, compliance disputes) since 2024?", "answer": "The AI Office runs GPAI enforcement directly, with powers to request technical documentation, run evaluations, demand corrective steps and issue fines, while national competent authorities take on other AI systems operating within their borders. Fines reach the higher of €15 million or 3 percent of the provider's worldwide annual turnover, and the Commission can also request a provider to restrict the making available on the market, withdraw or recall the model. The obligations applied from 2 August 2025, but the enforcement powers only became usable on 2 August 2026, and the AI Office has said it may use these powers in cases where technical compliance dialogues are not sufficient. I found no reported fine, formal investigation, or withdrawal order against a GPAI provider as of early October 2026, though my searches were limited and this rests on law-firm and commentary sources rather than an official enforcement register. On the market side, Meta said it won't be signing the Code, calling it introducing \"a number of legal uncertainties for model developers\", while OpenAI and Mistral have already signed the code. Sources disagree on signatory counts (one says more than 180 companies, another says roughly two dozen total signatories), and I could not find a source describing the AI Board's specific enforcement role.", "sources": [{"n": 18, "title": "EU begins enforcing AI Act, putting AI models under the microscope - Help Net Security", "quotes": ["The AI Office handles general-purpose AI models directly, with power to request technical documentation, run evaluations, demand corrective steps and ...", "National competent authorities take on other AI systems operating within their borders."]}, {"n": 19, "title": "EU AI Act Enforcement Phase Begins", "quotes": ["These powers are significant and include requesting information and documentation, obtaining access to models for evaluation, requiring corrective or ...", "The substantive obligations for providers of GPAI models placed on the market on or after August 2, 2025, have been applicable since that date, althou..."]}, {"n": 20, "title": "Frequently Asked Questions", "quotes": ["The powers include requesting information, requesting access to a model for evaluations, requiring risk mitigation measures, and issuing fines of up t...", "**The AI Office may use these powers in cases where technical compliance dialogues are not sufficient."]}, {"n": 21, "title": "Meta declines to sign EU AI code of practice", "quotes": ["We have carefully reviewed the European Commission's Code of Practice for general-purpose AI (GPAI) models and Meta won't be signing it,\" Kaplan said ...", "OpenAI and Mistral have already signed the code."]}, {"n": 22, "title": "Meta Skipped the EU AI Code: What Llama Dep… · AI Policy Desk", "quotes": ["More than 180 companies signed the Code of Practice to earn presumptive compliance."]}, {"n": 23, "title": "The EU AI Act GPAI Code of Practice: What It Is and ...", "quotes": ["As of this writing, signatories include major general-purpose model providers **Amazon, Anthropic, Google, IBM, Microsoft, Mistral AI, and OpenAI**, a..."]}], "confidence": 0.5, "reviewer_note": ""}, {"question": "What are the key dates and transitional timelines for GPAI obligations under the EU AI Act, including entry into force, the August 2025 application date for GPAI rules, and grandfathering provisions for existing models?", "answer": "The AI Act entered into force on 1 August 2024, and its general-purpose AI (GPAI) chapter began to apply on 2 August 2025, with Chapter V, the governance chapters, and the Chapter XII penalty framework taking effect that day. Models placed on the market from 2 August 2025 onward have to comply immediately. Models already on the market before 2 August 2025 must ensure compliance by 2 August 2027. The Commission could not enforce these duties until 2 August 2026, when its fining powers also became active, with fines of up to the greater of either €15 million or 3% of a company's global annual turnover. Sources conflict on fine timing for legacy models: Jones Day reads the rules as fines applying from 2 August 2026 for models where both original and modified versions were placed on the EU market after 2 August 2025, and from 2 August 2027 where both unmodified and modified versions were on the market on or before 2 August 2025, and it says it is unclear which regime covers legacy models modified after August 2025. Separately, the Commission's guidelines say legacy models need not be retrained or \"unlearned\" where that is impossible or disproportionate, but providers must disclose and justify this in their copyright policy and training-content summary.", "sources": [{"n": 4, "title": "EU AI Act: what applies from 2 August 2026 (GPAI enforcement + Article 50)", "quotes": ["| Article 113(a) | | 2 August 2025 | Chapter V (general-purpose AI models), governance chapters, and the Chapter XII penalty framework apply."]}, {"n": 5, "title": "EU rules on general-purpose AI models start to apply, bringing more transparency, safety and accountability", "quotes": ["Models already on the market before 2 August 2025 must ensure compliance by 2 August 2027."]}, {"n": 6, "title": "EU AI Act News: Rules on General-Purpose AI Start Applying, Guidelines and Template for Summary of Training Data Finalized", "quotes": ["The Commission can only take enforcement action from 2 August 2026, but this does not affect the applicability of obligations from 2 August 2025."]}, {"n": 7, "title": "EU AI Act: European Commission Publishes General-Purpose AI Code of Practice", "quotes": ["As from 2 August 2026, failure to satisfy the AI Act's requirements for GPAI models may result in substantial penalties, including the greater of eith...", "These fines will apply: From 2 August 2026, for GPAI models where both original and modified versions were placed on the EU market after 2 August 2025..."]}], "confidence": 0.72, "reviewer_note": "Explicitly flags an unresolved conflict (Jones Day) on which fine-timing regime applies to modified legacy models, without resolving it."}], "briefing": "On enforcement, the AI Office (not national authorities) directly oversees GPAI compliance, with power to demand documentation, run evaluations, and impose fines of up to 7% of global turnover [20]. As of early October 2026, no fine, investigation, or withdrawal order against a GPAI provider has been reported, though this is based on limited commentary rather than an official register [18][19]. The AI Office opened its first formal investigation into a GPAI provider in September 2026 [19]."}}
{"id": "grounded-q-bechtel-real", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 3, "why": "Real briefing text. All in finding 6, but the 'questions about cost and the 2030 schedule' come from [35], which is not cited; [32][33] cover the split and rebid. Right finding, less specific source.", "material": {"topic": "what limits deployment of small modular reactors in the US", "expect": "Cost and first-of-a-kind risk, NRC licensing, fuel supply (HALEU), and named projects including at least one that was cancelled or delayed.", "findings": [{"question": "Why did Bechtel cease involvement with TerraPower's Natrium project, and how does this affect the project's cost or schedule?", "answer": "Bechtel and TerraPower said they could not agree on the next phase of the Natrium project, so they decided to proceed separately, but neither has explained the disagreement in detail. The split was disclosed in public filings in Virginia, after the companies failed to agree on the next stages. Bechtel's work so far was done under an earlier phase of its agreement with TerraPower, before the project entered full EPC scope. Bechtel will stay on to finish the Sodium Test and Fill Facility, and TerraPower said it will rebid the engineering, procurement and construction contract for the estimated $4-billion project. On cost, the only figure reported is that $4 billion estimate, which dates to the Associated Press reporting in 2024 that the cost to build Natrium would be up to $4 billion. One trade publication warns that Bechtel's departure raises hard questions about Natrium's 2030 timeline and whether costs will balloon as TerraPower hunts for a replacement contractor, but that is analysis rather than a reported overrun, and I found no new cost estimate.", "sources": [{"n": 32, "title": "TerraPower, Bechtel part ways on the Kemmerer Natrium project - ExchangeMonitor", "quotes": ["after failing to come to an agreement on the next stages, according to public disclosure papers filed in Virginia."]}, {"n": 33, "title": "TerraPower Reopens EPC Competition for $4B Natrium Nuclear Project", "quotes": ["Bechtel spokeswoman Molly Edwards said the contractor's work to date was performed under an earlier phase of its agreement with TerraPower, before the...", "# TerraPower Reopens EPC Competition for $4B Natrium Nuclear Project ## Longtime contractor Bechtel exits full phase work five months after nuclear co..."]}, {"n": 34, "title": "Bechtel breaks ground on $4B Bill Gates-backed nuclear reactor", "quotes": ["- The Associated Press reported in 2024 that the cost to build Natrium would be up to $4 billion."]}, {"n": 35, "title": "Bechtel Exits TerraPower, Holtec Delays IPO: Nuclear Sector", "quotes": ["Bechtel's departure raises hard questions about Natrium's 2030 timeline and whether costs will balloon as TerraPower hunts for a replacement contracto..."]}], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "The core causal question (why) is explicitly unanswered by the sources; cost figure cited ($4B) predates the split and schedule impact is speculative analysis, not reported fact."}, {"question": "What are the cost, supply chain, and manufacturing challenges (e.g., HALEU fuel availability, forging capacity, first-of-a-kind cost overruns) currently constraining SMR projects in the US?", "answer": "**Fuel is the binding constraint, and HALEU is the main one.** Centrus, the only NRC-licensed HALEU producer, finished its demonstration production under a DOE contract in June 2026 and is targeting 12 metric tons of HALEU per year from new cascades starting in 2029. Against that, one industry tally puts current US production at about 900 kg/yr, and DOE has said HALEU shortages threaten timelines for NuScale, Oklo, TerraPower, and X-energy. TerraPower's Natrium shows the cost: its commercial-operation target moved from 2028 to 2030-31 primarily because of fuel availability.\n\n**Forging and manufacturing capacity is a weaker, less-documented constraint.** One analysis reports acute shortages in skilled labor and domestic forging capacity, but it is a secondary source and I found no primary data on forging throughput. It also describes the supply chain as unwilling to invest in new factories without firm orders, which is a demand-side problem rather than a fixed capacity limit.\n\n**First-of-a-kind cost overruns are the central economic risk, and the numbers conflict.** NuScale's Utah project saw its target power price climb from $55 per megawatt-hour to $89 before utilities withdrew in 2023. Published construction-cost estimates range from about $4,000 to $18,000 per kilowatt, and an IEEFA-cited TVA filing implies an estimated overnight cost for the BWRX-300 of $17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars.\n\n**The first US project's cost remains unsettled.** Earlier reporting put the first Clinch River unit at about $5.4 billion before interest and tax credits, but after the NRC's construction permit, coverage says TVA has not published an official cost estimate for Clinch River. TVA also said it continues negotiations with industry partners to ensure responsible cost sharing, and no construction start date has been announced.\n\n**Federal money is targeting these gaps, but it is not yet proven.** DOE's January 2026 announcement of $2.7 billion in HALEU and LEU enrichment task orders is aimed at fuel supply, and DOE separately is backing TVA with up to $400 million in cost-shared funding. Most of the evidence above comes from advocacy-oriented or secondary sources, so the timelines and cost figures should be treated as provisional.", "sources": [{"n": 7, "title": "BASELOAD SPECIAL: SMALL MODULAR REACTORS -- Safety, Hazards, Applications", "quotes": ["## Fuel supply Centrus Energy, the only NRC-licensed HALEU producer, finished its demonstration production under a DOE contract in June 2026 and is ta...", "The commercial-operation target moved from 2028 to 2030-31 primarily because of fuel availability.", "NuScale’s Utah project saw its target power price climb from $55 per megawatt-hour to $89 before members of the Utah Associated Municipal Power System..."]}, {"n": 8, "title": "State of Small Modular Reactors 2026 — Annual Intelligence Report", "quotes": ["Current US Production ~900 kg/yr"]}, {"n": 9, "title": "Financing the Nuclear Renaissance, Part II : How SMRs Will Actually Be Paid For - Oil & Gas 360", "quotes": ["**Designs dependent on HALEU with no diversified supply route** When DOE itself says HALEU shortages “threaten timelines for NuScale, Oklo, TerraPowe...", "**The HALEU Funding Push: Billions To Solve One Problem** DOE’s January 2026 announcement — awarding $2.7 billion in HALEU and LEU enrichment task or..."]}, {"n": 10, "title": "X-energy Nuclear 2026, $700M for SMRs, Amazon Deal - ENKI", "quotes": ["| Acute shortages in skilled labor and domestic forging capacity are exposed.", "This has created a \\\"chicken-and-egg\\\" problem where suppliers are unwilling to invest in new factories"]}, {"n": 11, "title": "Rush to Build New Nuclear Power: TVA and Administration Ignore Cost and Safety - Southern Alliance for Clean Energy (SACE)", "quotes": ["According to Schlissel’s testimony, TVA’s recent filings reveal an estimated overnight cost for the BWRX-300 of $17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars. ..."]}, {"n": 12, "title": "Tennessee Valley Authority Advances Plans to Build a Small Modular Reactor at Clinch River Site", "quotes": ["- The first unit could come online around 2032, add 300 megawatts of electricity, and cost about $5.4 billion before interest and tax credits."]}, {"n": 13, "title": "Green Light for America's First Small Modular Reactor", "quotes": ["TVA has not published an official cost estimate for Clinch River."]}, {"n": 14, "title": "NRC Construction Permit to TVA for BWRX-300", "quotes": ["The Tennessee Valley Authority (TVA) achieved a nationally significant milestone in America’s nuclear energy future with the U.S. ... ## NRC Construc..."]}], "confidence": 0.5, "reviewer_note": "Cost-per-kW estimates range implausibly widely ($4,000-$18,000/kW) with no reconciliation; forging capacity claim rests on a single secondary source with no primary data; overall framed as provisional by the answer itself."}], "briefing": "Execution risk compounds the above: Bechtel's exit from the Natrium EPC contract in 2025/26, with the companies unable to agree on next-phase terms, forces TerraPower to rebid a ~$4 billion contract, raising unresolved questions about cost and the 2030 schedule [32][33]."}}
{"id": "grounded-q-bechtel-fixed", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 4, "why": "Real paragraph with the schedule clause cited to [35]: each claim now points at the source that quotes it.", "material": {"topic": "what limits deployment of small modular reactors in the US", "expect": "Cost and first-of-a-kind risk, NRC licensing, fuel supply (HALEU), and named projects including at least one that was cancelled or delayed.", "findings": [{"question": "Why did Bechtel cease involvement with TerraPower's Natrium project, and how does this affect the project's cost or schedule?", "answer": "Bechtel and TerraPower said they could not agree on the next phase of the Natrium project, so they decided to proceed separately, but neither has explained the disagreement in detail. The split was disclosed in public filings in Virginia, after the companies failed to agree on the next stages. Bechtel's work so far was done under an earlier phase of its agreement with TerraPower, before the project entered full EPC scope. Bechtel will stay on to finish the Sodium Test and Fill Facility, and TerraPower said it will rebid the engineering, procurement and construction contract for the estimated $4-billion project. On cost, the only figure reported is that $4 billion estimate, which dates to the Associated Press reporting in 2024 that the cost to build Natrium would be up to $4 billion. One trade publication warns that Bechtel's departure raises hard questions about Natrium's 2030 timeline and whether costs will balloon as TerraPower hunts for a replacement contractor, but that is analysis rather than a reported overrun, and I found no new cost estimate.", "sources": [{"n": 32, "title": "TerraPower, Bechtel part ways on the Kemmerer Natrium project - ExchangeMonitor", "quotes": ["after failing to come to an agreement on the next stages, according to public disclosure papers filed in Virginia."]}, {"n": 33, "title": "TerraPower Reopens EPC Competition for $4B Natrium Nuclear Project", "quotes": ["Bechtel spokeswoman Molly Edwards said the contractor's work to date was performed under an earlier phase of its agreement with TerraPower, before the...", "# TerraPower Reopens EPC Competition for $4B Natrium Nuclear Project ## Longtime contractor Bechtel exits full phase work five months after nuclear co..."]}, {"n": 34, "title": "Bechtel breaks ground on $4B Bill Gates-backed nuclear reactor", "quotes": ["- The Associated Press reported in 2024 that the cost to build Natrium would be up to $4 billion."]}, {"n": 35, "title": "Bechtel Exits TerraPower, Holtec Delays IPO: Nuclear Sector", "quotes": ["Bechtel's departure raises hard questions about Natrium's 2030 timeline and whether costs will balloon as TerraPower hunts for a replacement contracto..."]}], "confidence": 0.4, "reviewer_note": "The core causal question (why) is explicitly unanswered by the sources; cost figure cited ($4B) predates the split and schedule impact is speculative analysis, not reported fact."}, {"question": "What are the cost, supply chain, and manufacturing challenges (e.g., HALEU fuel availability, forging capacity, first-of-a-kind cost overruns) currently constraining SMR projects in the US?", "answer": "**Fuel is the binding constraint, and HALEU is the main one.** Centrus, the only NRC-licensed HALEU producer, finished its demonstration production under a DOE contract in June 2026 and is targeting 12 metric tons of HALEU per year from new cascades starting in 2029. Against that, one industry tally puts current US production at about 900 kg/yr, and DOE has said HALEU shortages threaten timelines for NuScale, Oklo, TerraPower, and X-energy. TerraPower's Natrium shows the cost: its commercial-operation target moved from 2028 to 2030-31 primarily because of fuel availability.\n\n**Forging and manufacturing capacity is a weaker, less-documented constraint.** One analysis reports acute shortages in skilled labor and domestic forging capacity, but it is a secondary source and I found no primary data on forging throughput. It also describes the supply chain as unwilling to invest in new factories without firm orders, which is a demand-side problem rather than a fixed capacity limit.\n\n**First-of-a-kind cost overruns are the central economic risk, and the numbers conflict.** NuScale's Utah project saw its target power price climb from $55 per megawatt-hour to $89 before utilities withdrew in 2023. Published construction-cost estimates range from about $4,000 to $18,000 per kilowatt, and an IEEFA-cited TVA filing implies an estimated overnight cost for the BWRX-300 of $17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars.\n\n**The first US project's cost remains unsettled.** Earlier reporting put the first Clinch River unit at about $5.4 billion before interest and tax credits, but after the NRC's construction permit, coverage says TVA has not published an official cost estimate for Clinch River. TVA also said it continues negotiations with industry partners to ensure responsible cost sharing, and no construction start date has been announced.\n\n**Federal money is targeting these gaps, but it is not yet proven.** DOE's January 2026 announcement of $2.7 billion in HALEU and LEU enrichment task orders is aimed at fuel supply, and DOE separately is backing TVA with up to $400 million in cost-shared funding. Most of the evidence above comes from advocacy-oriented or secondary sources, so the timelines and cost figures should be treated as provisional.", "sources": [{"n": 7, "title": "BASELOAD SPECIAL: SMALL MODULAR REACTORS -- Safety, Hazards, Applications", "quotes": ["## Fuel supply Centrus Energy, the only NRC-licensed HALEU producer, finished its demonstration production under a DOE contract in June 2026 and is ta...", "The commercial-operation target moved from 2028 to 2030-31 primarily because of fuel availability.", "NuScale’s Utah project saw its target power price climb from $55 per megawatt-hour to $89 before members of the Utah Associated Municipal Power System..."]}, {"n": 8, "title": "State of Small Modular Reactors 2026 — Annual Intelligence Report", "quotes": ["Current US Production ~900 kg/yr"]}, {"n": 9, "title": "Financing the Nuclear Renaissance, Part II : How SMRs Will Actually Be Paid For - Oil & Gas 360", "quotes": ["**Designs dependent on HALEU with no diversified supply route** When DOE itself says HALEU shortages “threaten timelines for NuScale, Oklo, TerraPowe...", "**The HALEU Funding Push: Billions To Solve One Problem** DOE’s January 2026 announcement — awarding $2.7 billion in HALEU and LEU enrichment task or..."]}, {"n": 10, "title": "X-energy Nuclear 2026, $700M for SMRs, Amazon Deal - ENKI", "quotes": ["| Acute shortages in skilled labor and domestic forging capacity are exposed.", "This has created a \\\"chicken-and-egg\\\" problem where suppliers are unwilling to invest in new factories"]}, {"n": 11, "title": "Rush to Build New Nuclear Power: TVA and Administration Ignore Cost and Safety - Southern Alliance for Clean Energy (SACE)", "quotes": ["According to Schlissel’s testimony, TVA’s recent filings reveal an estimated overnight cost for the BWRX-300 of $17,949 per kilowatt in 2024 dollars. ..."]}, {"n": 12, "title": "Tennessee Valley Authority Advances Plans to Build a Small Modular Reactor at Clinch River Site", "quotes": ["- The first unit could come online around 2032, add 300 megawatts of electricity, and cost about $5.4 billion before interest and tax credits."]}, {"n": 13, "title": "Green Light for America's First Small Modular Reactor", "quotes": ["TVA has not published an official cost estimate for Clinch River."]}, {"n": 14, "title": "NRC Construction Permit to TVA for BWRX-300", "quotes": ["The Tennessee Valley Authority (TVA) achieved a nationally significant milestone in America’s nuclear energy future with the U.S. ... ## NRC Construc..."]}], "confidence": 0.5, "reviewer_note": "Cost-per-kW estimates range implausibly widely ($4,000-$18,000/kW) with no reconciliation; forging ca