{"id": "grounded-q-xid-real", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 2, "why": "Real briefing text. 'Catastrophic data loss ... bytes remain on disk' is cited to [1][3]; that is quoted from [2], and [3]'s quote is about refusing writes at 100% utilisation, a different claim. A citation to the wrong source.", "material": {"topic": "how PostgreSQL prevents transaction ID wraparound", "expect": "32-bit transaction IDs, freezing old tuples, autovacuum and anti-wraparound vacuum, the relevant settings (autovacuum_freeze_max_age), and what happens if it is ignored.", "findings": [{"question": "What is the PostgreSQL transaction ID (XID) wraparound problem and why does it threaten data integrity?", "answer": "PostgreSQL's transaction IDs are 32-bit counters that wrap around every 4 billion transactions, and XIDs underpin its MVCC concurrency and streaming replication (). The danger is that visibility depends on comparing XIDs, so after a wrap, older committed rows would seem to belong to the future and disappear from queries; the official docs call this catastrophic data loss, though the bytes remain on disk (). To prevent this, VACUUM marks old rows as frozen so they count as visible to all transactions, and the docs say every table in every database must be vacuumed at least once every two billion transactions. As a backstop, PostgreSQL stops accepting write commands near the limit: Google's Cloud SQL documentation says that at 100% utilization PostgreSQL stops accepting write queries, and the official docs say a safety margin of one million transactions lets an administrator run VACUUM manually. The sources do not fully agree on details: the 9.4 docs describe the wrap as occurring at 2³² with a 2-billion-transaction vacuum deadline, while the 8.2 docs say normal XIDs are compared using modulo-2^31 arithmetic, and the current docs I could retrieve were only partial, so I could not confirm the present-day wording.", "sources": [{"n": 1, "title": "PostgreSQL: Documentation: 19: 67.1. Transactions and Identifiers", "quotes": ["Transactions and Identifiers # The internal transaction ID type xid is 32 bits wide and wraps around every 4 billion transactions.", "Xids are used as the basis for PostgreSQL's MVCC concurrency mechanism and streaming replication."]}, {"n": 2, "title": "PostgreSQL: Documentation: 9.4: Routine Vacuuming", "quotes": ["In short, catastrophic data loss.", "(Actually the data is still there, but that's cold comfort if you cannot get at it.)", "To avoid this, it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions."]}, {"n": 3, "title": "Prevent transaction ID wraparound", "quotes": ["Once the transaction ID utilization percentage reaches 100%, PostgreSQL stops accepting write queries."]}, {"n": 4, "title": "PostgreSQL: Documentation: 8.2: Routine Vacuuming", "quotes": ["Normal XIDs are compared using modulo-2^31 arithmetic."]}], "confidence": 0.55, "reviewer_note": "Core mechanism (MVCC visibility, freezing) is right, but the answer admits it could not confirm the current (v18) docs wording and instead leans on 9.4 and 8.2 docs, leaving the present-day phrasing and exact thresholds unverified; also cites a 'docs/19' URL for a version whose existence/content is not established in this answer."}, {"question": "What happens when a database approaches or reaches wraparound (e.g., emergency autovacuum, read-only mode), and how can DBAs detect and respond to warnings?", "answer": "PostgreSQL forces freezing in stages: once a table's oldest unfrozen XID age passes autovacuum_freeze_max_age (200 million by default), it launches an anti-wraparound autovacuum even when autovacuum is disabled, and vacuum_failsafe_age (default 1.6 billion multixacts) is VACUUM's strategy of last resort, skipping cost delays and non-essential work like index vacuuming (cost-based delay is no longer applied, and further non-essential maintenance tasks are bypassed). Per the official documentation, the system begins emitting warnings when the database's oldest XIDs reach forty million transactions from the wraparound point, and if those are ignored, it stops accepting commands that assign new transaction IDs near the three-million-remaining mark. Reads keep working in that state, so \"read-only\" is imprecise, and one blog's claim that PostgreSQL 19 raises the warning point to 100 million comes from a single source and is unverified. Sources also conflict on the numbers: Percona's guide says a countdown begins at 10 million remaining and read-only mode at 1 million, which doesn't match the official figures for versions 14–18. For detection, monitor age(datfrozenxid) per database and age(relfrozenxid) per table, alerting well before the limit (one guide suggests alerting at 50% of autovacuum_freeze_max_age), and if XID age has been climbing for days, autovacuum is falling behind and you need to investigate why. For response, the official docs recommend a database-wide VACUUM rather than VACUUM FULL, which would fail, and avoiding single-user mode on modern versions; blockers such as long-running transactions and stale prepared transactions or replication slots should also be cleared.", "sources": [{"n": 9, "title": "PostgreSQL: Documentation: 18: 19.10. Vacuuming", "quotes": ["Freezing # **vacuum\\_failsafe\\_age (integer) #**: Specifies the maximum age (in transactions) that a table's pg\\_class.relfrozenxid field can attain b...", "When the failsafe is triggered, any cost-based delay that is in effect will no longer be applied, and further non-essential maintenance tasks (such as..."]}, {"n": 11, "title": "PostgreSQL: Documentation: 18: 24.1. Routine Vacuuming", "quotes": ["If for some reason autovacuum fails to clear old XIDs from a table, the system will begin to emit warning messages like this when the database's oldes...", "``` ``` ERROR: database is not accepting commands that assign new transaction IDs to avoid wraparound data loss in database \"mydb\" HINT: Execute a ..."]}, {"n": 12, "title": "PostgreSQL frozen XID monitoring: catching wraparound 6 months early", "quotes": ["The primary scalar for wraparound risk: ```sql SELECT datname, age(datfrozenxid) FROM pg_database ORDER BY 2 DESC; ``` ## Alert thresholds and tiere..."]}, {"n": 13, "title": "PostgreSQL XID Wraparound: What It Is and How to Prevent It - DEV Community", "quotes": ["If XID age has been climbing for days, autovacuum is falling behind and you need to investigate why."]}], "confidence": 0.65, "reviewer_note": "Core thresholds are grounded in official docs and match Finding 6, and conflicting vendor claims (Percona, PG19 100M) are appropriately flagged as unverified rather than asserted; minor weakness is the vacuum_failsafe_age default described as '1.6 billion multixacts' without clarifying units/version precisely."}], "briefing": "The core problem: PostgreSQL's XIDs are 32-bit counters underpinning MVCC visibility, and after ~4 billion transactions they wrap around; without intervention, old committed rows would appear to be from the future and effectively vanish from query results — the official docs call this catastrophic data loss, even though the underlying bytes remain on disk [1][3]. The fix is freezing: VACUUM marks old tuples as permanently visible to all transactions, and documentation has long required every table to be vacuumed at least once every two billion transactions [2][4] (note: older 8.2/9.4 docs describe slightly different wraparound arithmetic, so exact wording has evolved) [2][4]."}}
{"id": "grounded-q-xid-fixed", "criterion": "grounded", "label": 4, "why": "Real paragraph with citations corrected: data loss to [1][2], the two-billion rule to [2], the older-docs note to [2][4].", "material": {"topic": "how PostgreSQL prevents transaction ID wraparound", "expect": "32-bit transaction IDs, freezing old tuples, autovacuum and anti-wraparound vacuum, the relevant settings (autovacuum_freeze_max_age), and what happens if it is ignored.", "findings": [{"question": "What is the PostgreSQL transaction ID (XID) wraparound problem and why does it threaten data integrity?", "answer": "PostgreSQL's transaction IDs are 32-bit counters that wrap around every 4 billion transactions, and XIDs underpin its MVCC concurrency and streaming replication (). The danger is that visibility depends on comparing XIDs, so after a wrap, older committed rows would seem to belong to the future and disappear from queries; the official docs call this catastrophic data loss, though the bytes remain on disk (). To prevent this, VACUUM marks old rows as frozen so they count as visible to all transactions, and the docs say every table in every database must be vacuumed at least once every two billion transactions. As a backstop, PostgreSQL stops accepting write commands near the limit: Google's Cloud SQL documentation says that at 100% utilization PostgreSQL stops accepting write queries, and the official docs say a safety margin of one million transactions lets an administrator run VACUUM manually. The sources do not fully agree on details: the 9.4 docs describe the wrap as occurring at 2³² with a 2-billion-transaction vacuum deadline, while the 8.2 docs say normal XIDs are compared using modulo-2^31 arithmetic, and the current docs I could retrieve were only partial, so I could not confirm the present-day wording.", "sources": [{"n": 1, "title": "PostgreSQL: Documentation: 19: 67.1. Transactions and Identifiers", "quotes": ["Transactions and Identifiers # The internal transaction ID type xid is 32 bits wide and wraps around every 4 billion transactions.", "Xids are used as the basis for PostgreSQL's MVCC concurrency mechanism and streaming replication."]}, {"n": 2, "title": "PostgreSQL: Documentation: 9.4: Routine Vacuuming", "quotes": ["In short, catastrophic data loss.", "(Actually the data is still there, but that's cold comfort if you cannot get at it.)", "To avoid this, it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions."]}, {"n": 3, "title": "Prevent transaction ID wraparound", "quotes": ["Once the transaction ID utilization percentage reaches 100%, PostgreSQL stops accepting write queries."]}, {"n": 4, "title": "PostgreSQL: Documentation: 8.2: Routine Vacuuming", "quotes": ["Normal XIDs are compared using modulo-2^31 arithmetic."]}], "confidence": 0.55, "reviewer_note": "Core mechanism (MVCC visibility, freezing) is right, but the answer admits it could not confirm the current (v18) docs wording and instead leans on 9.4 and 8.2 docs, leaving the present-day phrasing and exact thresholds unverified; also cites a 'docs/19' URL for a version whose existence/content is not established in this answer."}, {"question": "What happens when a database approaches or reaches wraparound (e.g., emergency autovacuum, read-only mode), and how can DBAs detect and respond to warnings?", "answer": "PostgreSQL forces freezing in stages: once a table's oldest unfrozen XID age passes autovacuum_freeze_max_age (200 million by default), it launches an anti-wraparound autovacuum even when autovacuum is disabled, and vacuum_failsafe_age (default 1.6 billion multixacts) is VACUUM's strategy of last resort, skipping cost delays and non-essential work like index vacuuming (cost-based delay is no longer applied, and further non-essential maintenance tasks are bypassed). Per the official documentation, the system begins emitting warnings when the database's oldest XIDs reach forty million transactions from the wraparound point, and if those are ignored, it stops accepting commands that assign new transaction IDs near the three-million-remaining mark. Reads keep working in that state, so \"read-only\" is imprecise, and one blog's claim that PostgreSQL 19 raises the warning point to 100 million comes from a single source and is unverified. Sources also conflict on the numbers: Percona's guide says a countdown begins at 10 million remaining and read-only mode at 1 million, which doesn't match the official figures for versions 14–18. For detection, monitor age(datfrozenxid) per database and age(relfrozenxid) per table, alerting well before the limit (one guide suggests alerting at 50% of autovacuum_freeze_max_age), and if XID age has been climbing for days, autovacuum is falling behind and you need to investigate why. For response, the official docs recommend a database-wide VACUUM rather than VACUUM FULL, which would fail, and avoiding single-user mode on modern versions; blockers such as long-running transactions and stale prepared transactions or replication slots should also be cleared.", "sources": [{"n": 9, "title": "PostgreSQL: Documentation: 18: 19.10. Vacuuming", "quotes": ["Freezing # **vacuum\\_failsafe\\_age (integer) #**: Specifies the maximum age (in transactions) that a table's pg\\_class.relfrozenxid field can attain b...", "When the failsafe is triggered, any cost-based delay that is in effect will no longer be applied, and further non-essential maintenance tasks (such as..."]}, {"n": 11, "title": "PostgreSQL: Documentation: 18: 24.1. Routine Vacuuming", "quotes": ["If for some reason autovacuum fails to clear old XIDs from a table, the system will begin to emit warning messages like this when the database's oldes...", "``` ``` ERROR: database is not accepting commands that assign new transaction IDs to avoid wraparound data loss in database \"mydb\" HINT: Execute a ..."]}, {"n": 12, "title": "PostgreSQL frozen XID monitoring: catching wraparound 6 months early", "quotes": ["The primary scalar for wraparound risk: ```sql SELECT datname, age(datfrozenxid) FROM pg_database ORDER BY 2 DESC; ``` ## Alert thresholds and tiere..."]}, {"n": 13, "title": "PostgreSQL XID Wraparound: What It Is and How to Prevent It - DEV Community", "quotes": ["If XID age has been climbing for days, autovacuum is falling behind and you need to investigate why."]}], "confidence": 0.65, "reviewer_note": "Core thresholds are grounded in official docs and match Finding 6, and conflicting vendor claims (Percona, PG19 100M) are appropriately flagged as unverified rather than asserted; minor weakness is the vacuum_failsafe_age default described as '1.6 billion multixacts' without clarifying units/version precisely."}], "briefing": "The core problem: PostgreSQL's XIDs are 32-bit counters underpinning MVCC visibility, and after ~4 billion transactions they wrap around; without intervention, old committed rows would appear to be from the future and effectively vanish from query results — the official docs call this catastrophic data loss, even though the underlying bytes remain on disk [1][2]. The fix is freezing: VACUUM marks old tuples as permanently visible to all transactions, and documentation has long required every table to be vacuumed at least once every two billion transactions [2] (note: older 8.2/9.4 docs describe slightly different wraparound arithmetic, so exact wording has evolved) [2][4]."}}
